    "collected_at": "2026-11-02T13:58:31.308744+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/iconic-jdm-sat-28-years-in-barn-owner-s-family-lets-it-go-at-less-than-half-price-revival-incoming-277862.html",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.autoevolution.com/news/iconic-jdm-sat-28-years-in-barn-owner-s-family-lets-it-go-at-less-than-half-price-revival-incoming-277862.html",
    "origin_publisher": "autoevolution",
    "published_at": "2026-11-02T12:42:13+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-02T12:42:13+0000",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-02T14:01:53.705309+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-02T14:01:53.705309+00:00",
    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • Toyota Puts Snow Tracks on Five SUVs and Trucks, Unloads Them at SEMA 2026 as the Snowhunter Bunch\nIconic JDM Sat 28 Years in Barn, Owner's Family Lets It Go at Less Than Half Price, Revival Incoming\n63 photos\nPhoto: YouTube/WD Detailing\nProduced between 1983 and 2000 across two distinct generations, the Nissan 300ZX carried the Japanese automaker's Z-car sports coupe lineage from the analog 1980s into the high-tech 1990s. Known as the Fairlady Z in its home market, the 300ZX earned a permanent seat at the table of 1990s Japanese performance royalty alongside the Nissan Skyline GT-R, the fourth-generation Toyota Supra, the Mazda RX-7, and the aluminum-bodied mid-engine Honda NSX.\nWhile the Toyota Supra and Nissan Skyline GT-R later dominated popular culture through movie franchises and tuner-friendly inline-six engines capable of massive aftermarket output, the 300ZX carved a different legacy. Celebrated by period journalists as a technological tour de force with supercar styling, it remained slightly more niche in tuning circles only because its tightly packed V6 engine bay put off mechanics, making clean, unmolested survivors prized collector pieces today.\nOne such survivor is a yellow 1990 Nissan 300ZX 2+0 T-top coupe that spent 28 years hidden away inside a dark storage building after last seeing the road in 1998. To rescue the forgotten Japanese sports car and transform it into a daily driver, the Cleveland, Ohio specialists at WD Detailing, RJ Wagner, Brent DiCesare, and Mike Tudor, teamed up with YouTube’s most famous white racing helmet, the DIY automotive creator Chris Magello of the ChrisFix channel.\nAlthough Magello has spent 16 years teaching millions how to maintain and repair vehicles while wearing his trademark white racing helmet, this 1990 Z32 marked his very first barn-find project. Yes, after nearly two BILLION YouTube views, the car nut still has new things to do, learn, and teach.\nPhoto: YouTube/WD Detailing He spotted the 106,000-mile (170,590-kilometer) 5-speed manual coupe through AutoTempest on an eBay estate sale listing that included only three photos, a $4,900 asking price, and a short note stating the car ran when parked in 1998.\nMoving quickly, Magello asked his friend Hussein Ashkanani from Forza Automotive to inspect the Nissan that same day. After checking 5-year price trends showing clean naturally aspirated Z32 coupes listed between $9,000 and $10,000 and selling for $7,000 to $8,000, Magello negotiated a $2,300 purchase price by promising to get it running again.\nDuring the initial inspection, Hussein also revealed the dark, heartbreaking reason the sports car had been locked away.\nPhoto: YouTube/WD Detailing The woman selling the 300ZX had inherited it from her brother, who drove the car during the 1990s and ran a towing company. One night in 1998, he responded to a tow call, where the callers robbed and fatally shot him. Grief-stricken, his family parked the coupe inside their barn with its July 1998 Kentucky license plate, leaving it untouched on four flat tires until the YouTubers pulled it out for a f ull revival.\nChrisFix the 16-inch alloy wheels using rubber cleaner and iron-dissolving Purple Rain solution before the crew pressure-washed the engine bay and foamed the body with Cherry Suds. Hoisting the car on the lift, RJ put on a hazmat suit and racing helmet to degrease the undercarriage.\nInside the T-top cabin, Mike inspected the pod-style dashboard controls, door-mounted seat belts, and lifeless clutch pedal before pulling the seats, leaving Brent to treat the heavy carpet with Rust-Oleum rust dissolver and enzyme pre-soak. Once the car was nice and shiny, the livery got a thorough polish before the new owner took it to his shop for a mechanical overhaul (to be featured on his YouTube channel).\nPhoto: YouTube/WD Detailing Competing in the front-engine, rear-wheel-drive sports car and grand tourer segment, the Nissan 300ZX spanned two generations: the Z31 built from 1983 to 1989 and the Z32 produced from 1989 to 2000. Offered with 5-speed manual or 4-speed automatic transmissions, the Z31 replaced earlier inline-sixes with Japan's first mass-produced V6 engines, alongside a pair of Japanese-market 2.0-liter engines - an inline-six and a turbo V6 rated at 170 PS (168 hp).\nIn export markets, the first-generation Z31 launched for 1984 with the naturally aspirated 3.0-liter SOHC 12-valve V6, delivering 160 hp (162 PS) and 173 lb-ft (235 Nm) of torque for an 8.6-second 0 to 60 mph (97 kph) run and a 134 mph (216 kph) top speed. Conversely, the pinnacle Z32 engine was the 3.0-liter DOHC 24-valve twin-turbocharged V6, packing dual intercoolers and variable valve timing.\nThat flagship powerplant generated 300 hp (304 PS) at 6,400 rpm and 283 lb-ft (384 Nm) of torque at 3,600 rpm, launching the Z32 Twin Turbo from 0 to 60 mph (97 kph) in 5.0 seconds, through the quarter-mile in 13.7 seconds at 101 mph (163 kph), and to a limited 155 mph (250 kph) top speed. Out of roughly 270,000 Z32 units built globally, North America received approximately 83,000 cars.\nPhoto: YouTube/WD Detailing Magello's 1990 model hails from the Z32's inaugural and highest-selling North American season, which accounted for 39,290 units and earned Motor Trend's Import Car of the Year award. Measuring 169.5 inches (4,305 millimeters) long on a 96.5-inch (2,451-millimeter) wheelbase and weighing 3,219 lbs (1,460 kg), the 1990 300ZX coupe houses the naturally aspirated 3.0-liter (2,960-cc) DOHC 24-valve V6 producing 222 hp (225 PS) and 198 lb-ft (268 Nm).\nPaired with its factory 5-speed manual gearbox and four-wheel independent multi-link suspension, the 1990 naturally aspirated coupe reaches 60 mph (97 kph) in 7.0 seconds, covers the quarter-mile in 15.5 seconds at 91 mph (146 kph), and hits a 146 mph (235 kph) top speed. Now gleaming after its WD Detailing revival, this 1990 300ZX is ready for Magello's wrenching to return to the streets after 28 years.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nnissan 300zx barn find 1990 Nissan 300 ZX Nissan sportscar JDM WD Detailing car detailing\nAbout the author: Razvan Calin\nAfter nearly two decades in news television, Răzvan turned to a different medium. He’s been a field journalist, a TV producer, and a seafarer but found that he feels right at home among petrolheads. Full profile\nUP NEXT Restored 1964 Chevy Corvette Selling at No Reserve, Price Already Going Through the Roof",
